  263:   RE-ENTRY -- every exit returns before the slot's function pointer is replaced, so the slot stays untranslated.  Re-entry after
  264: arm (a)'s exit is measured by `T ra`: the slot stays untranslated and the real fetch re-enters the arm in the same batch (the
  265: second report).  Re-entry after arm (c)'s exit is untestable here: the park ends the batch and running is clear, so nothing
  266: fetches the slot again; the `again` session therefore measures nothing about re-entry after a park and stands as the control
  267: that the debugger survives step and continue on a halted CPU, its halt-count row the control that a -T halt is terminal for the
  268: session.  W5's catch rides on the uninitialised instruction word: which rows red under W5 depends on the garbage decoded, though
  269: some row must (a decoded word moves pc, traps or prints UNIMPLEMENTED; one that branches back to its own slot changes nothing on
  270: that session -- `T blr` stays green under R7b_f_me and R7b_f_ra -- so the claim rests on the other sessions).  -T imprecision: a -T
  271: data halt earlier in the same batch followed by a declined fetch takes arm (c), with no machine check (cosmetic; the debugger is
  272: entered either way). The reconciliation's W7 (the read-ahead guard missing) is rowed by `T ra`; its W8 (the running-1 arm missing)
  273: and W9 (a wrong machine check) have no row on pmppc: they need its ROW 5 (FILED pending X11).  On this 249-row probe the pre-fix
